(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-vignettes"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 44-year-old man admitted with Rocky Mountain spotted fever has blood tests sent. Which pattern is expected?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia, anaemia, mild hyponatraemia and mild transaminitis with a normal white count and increased bands","Leukocytosis with a left shift, raised inflammatory markers and a normal platelet count throughout","Eosinophilia with a raised immunoglobulin E level and otherwise normal chemistry","Entirely normal blood tests, with the diagnosis made on clinical grounds alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cerebrospinal fluid shows leukocytosis with moderately raised protein and normal glucose. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman in Connecticut removed a tick from her thigh eight days ago. She now has a 9 cm round red patch there with a paler centre and a darker dot in the middle, plus fever and aching. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stage 1 Lyme disease with erythema migrans","Cellulitis at the site of the tick bite","A local hypersensitivity reaction to tick saliva","Early Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a lesion over 5 cm with central clearing about a week after the bite, with constitutional symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has intense itching with linear excoriations across his back, neck, shoulders and waist, and patches of darker pigmentation where older lesions have healed. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pubic louse infestation","Scabies of the trunk and back","Body louse infestation","Head louse infestation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that distribution with post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases is characteristic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has an eruption whose reaction pattern has been recognised. Which characteristics are used next to narrow the differential?","choices":["Culture and sensitivity of the lesion together with a complete blood count and chemistry","Presence of pruritus, presence of pain, and whether regional lymph nodes are enlarged","Colour, shape, configuration of the lesions relative to one another, and their distribution on the body","Duration of the eruption, the patient's age, and whether any treatment has been tried so far"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those tune the differential the reaction pattern has already generated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl has an itchy scalp, and the school nurse found tiny pale specks firmly attached to the hair shafts that will not brush off. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies affecting the scalp and hairline","Head louse infestation","Seborrhoeic dermatitis of the scalp","Tinea capitis with scaling and breakage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nits cannot be removed from the hair shaft, which separates them from dandruff. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man in Oregon was bitten in his basement in August. He felt nothing at the time, then noticed hardness and tingling within half an hour, and a blister formed the next day. Which spider is most likely responsible?","choices":["A tarantula that had been kept as a pet","The hobo spider","The brown recluse spider","The black widow spider"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the Pacific Northwest, a painless bite, induration and paraesthesia within thirty minutes, vesicles by thirty-six hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a black widow spider bite is being treated for envenomation. Which additional measure should be checked?","choices":["That the wound has been debrided before he is admitted","That his tetanus vaccination is up to date","That he has had a course of prophylactic antibiotics","That an antivenom skin test has been performed first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside calcium gluconate, narcotics, muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman bitten by a hobo spider has fatigue, headache and difficulty concentrating a week later. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["These symptoms indicate an allergic reaction needing an antihistamine course","Headaches may persist for about a week and the symptoms are managed supportively","These symptoms indicate an evolving necrotic wound needing surgical review","These symptoms indicate a secondary infection requiring an oral antibiotic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has been prescribed permethrin for scabies. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Apply it overnight to the entire skin surface with attention to creases, and repeat the application one week later","Apply it overnight to the affected areas only, and no repeat application will be needed afterwards","Wash the skin vigorously with a strong soap before each application to remove the mites","Expect all itching to have stopped within three days, and return if any remains after that"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the second application is the point most often missed. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man had anaphylaxis after a wasp sting last summer and has a positive skin test. Which is the most appropriate long-term management?","choices":["Take a daily leukotriene antagonist through the summer months of each year","Avoid all outdoor activity between the months of July and September each year","Carry an epinephrine auto-injector and be referred for desensitisation therapy","Carry an oral antihistamine and take it at the first sign of any sting reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity as well. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with cutaneous larva migrans of the foot asks about treatment. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Topical permethrin to the affected area with a second application at one week","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days, or ivermectin for one to two days","Cryotherapy applied to the advancing end of the track where the larva is located","Surgical excision of the leading edge of the lesion under local anaesthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is less effective and surgery or cryotherapy is not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has a suspected serpentine skin lesion but the diagnosis is uncertain and follicular pustules are also present. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["Serology for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the responsible organism","Dermoscopy of the lesion looking for the ovoid eggs within the affected skin","Bacterial culture of an unroofed pustule sent for Gram stain and sensitivities","Light microscopy with mineral oil, which shows live and dead larvae in folliculitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the serpentine rash alone is enough for a clinical diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man from a rural area has fever, severe headache, nausea and calf pain for five days but no rash. Which is the most appropriate consideration?","choices":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever can present without a rash in about 20% of patients","The absence of rash excludes the diagnosis, so an alternative should be sought","The rash always appears within twenty-four hours of the fever beginning","Treatment should be withheld until a rash confirms the clinical diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the full triad is present in only about 60% and the rash follows the fever. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl needs treatment for Rocky Mountain spotted fever but her parents raise the usual concerns about doxycycline in young children. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Doxycycline is still used here, with a desensitisation protocol available where it is contraindicated","Doxycycline must be avoided under eight years of age, so azithromycin is substituted instead","Doxycycline must be avoided under eight years, so amoxicillin is substituted instead","Doxycycline should be delayed until the child is confirmed positive on serology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the risk of untreated disease outweighs the concern about tooth staining. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman handling her pet tarantula developed a red painful eye an hour later and now has blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral, since embedded hairs can cause corneal granuloma","Topical corticosteroid to the eyelid skin with review in one week's time","Oral antihistamine with artificial tears and reassessment in three days","Irrigation of the eye at home with saline and no further follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical corticosteroid addresses only the cutaneous reaction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy's parents want to avoid chemical treatment for head lice. Which physical approach can be offered, and with what caveat?","choices":["Applying mayonnaise or mineral oil weekly to smother the live lice and their attached eggs on the hair","Washing the hair daily with an antibacterial shampoo until no live lice are seen","Waiting without treatment, since head lice clear spontaneously within a few weeks","Combing nits out after two minutes of hair moisturiser every few days, though it is slow and needs adjuvant therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — head shaving is the other physical option named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has severe pain after an asp caterpillar sting that is not controlled by an oral analgesic. Which additional options are described?","choices":["A ten-day course of oral doxycycline to prevent secondary skin infection","Oral or parenteral narcotic analgesia, with antivenom for certain categories","Intravenous calcium gluconate with a muscle relaxant and a benzodiazepine","Intramuscular epinephrine repeated at intervals until the pain has settled"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside antihistamines, menthol or camphor, and corticosteroids. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a classic erythema migrans lesion after a tick bite in Vermont. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Send an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay and withhold treatment until it returns","Send a Western blot and treat only if the immunoglobulin G band is positive","Reassure and review in two weeks, since most tick bites need no treatment","Diagnose and treat him on clinical grounds without waiting for serology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a patient with the lesion is treated clinically, and testing is most useful outside endemic regions. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy who swims in the same lake each summer asks how to reduce his risk of swimmer's itch. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Take a prophylactic antiparasitic before each swimming session","Wash and dry the skin properly after leaving the water","Apply DEET-based repellent to the legs before entering the water","Wear closed footwear on the shore and avoid sitting on the sand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treatment otherwise is symptomatic when the eruption occurs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has confirmed bedbug bites and asks what will get rid of them. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Leaving the room unoccupied for a month will starve the insects out of the property","Vacuuming floors, carpets and upholstery thoroughly is sufficient without any chemical treatment","A professional exterminator is necessary; treatment of the bites themselves is symptomatic","Washing all bedding at 60 degrees Celsius and bagging clothing for fourteen days will clear it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical antiseptic or antibiotic for secondary infection, steroids or antihistamines for itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with confirmed tungiasis of the foot asks how it will be treated. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Symptomatic care with antihistamines and oatmeal baths until the lesions resolve","Surgical excision or cryotherapy with tetanus prophylaxis and systemic antibiotics","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days with no procedural treatment needed","Overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — dermoscopy showing ovoid eggs is what makes the diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy is stung by a honeybee and the stinger is still visible in the skin. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Apply ice for twenty minutes and remove it once the swelling has settled down","Leave it in place, since removal releases the remaining venom into the tissue","Scrape it off with a card edge held parallel to the skin, as quickly as possible","Grasp it with fine forceps and withdraw it with steady traction along its axis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the barbed ovipositor keeps pumping venom while it remains impaled. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man bitten by a brown recluse six days ago now has a black eschar over the site with a deep ulcer forming beneath it. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Intravenous calcium gluconate with muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines for the pain","Oral doxycycline for ten days to prevent the wound becoming secondarily infected","Pain control, warm compresses and avoiding strenuous exercise, deferring surgery until the wound is stable","Immediate wide excision of the necrotic area with primary closure of the resulting skin defect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — necrotic wounds heal very slowly and may later need reconstruction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man in Arizona, where Lyme disease is not endemic, has fatigue and joint aches and asks to be tested. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Testing is not indicated at all, since Lyme disease occurs only in the northeastern states of the country","Testing should be deferred until an erythema migrans lesion has actually appeared","Testing should be replaced by an empirical course of doxycycline for two weeks","Testing is most helpful in exactly this situation — outside an endemic region with possibly consistent symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay first, with Western blot more specific. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with suspected scabies has heavily excoriated hands and no intact burrow visible. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide wet mount of a plucked hair examined under the microscope","Serology for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the causative mite","A bacterial culture taken from an excoriated area and sent for sensitivities","The burrow ink test, looking for a zigzag line running across and away from the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — blue or black ink is applied to a suspected lesion when scraping is difficult. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with Lyme arthritis of the knee has not responded to a full course of oral doxycycline. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["A second oral course of doxycycline for a further fortnight","Oral azithromycin, since a macrolide covers resistant organisms","Intra-articular corticosteroid injection into the affected knee","Intravenous ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or penicillin G"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — intravenous therapy is used for arthritis and acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man is being treated for Rocky Mountain spotted fever and asks how long the course will last. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Amoxicillin three times daily for a course of ten to fourteen days","Azithromycin once daily for a course of five days, then reassessment","Doxycycline 100 mg by mouth every twelve hours for five to ten days","Doxycycline 100 mg by mouth every twelve hours for four to six weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same agent is used in pregnancy, and children get a weight-based dose. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has suspected scabies with several unexcoriated papules on the wrist. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Serological testing for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the mite","Punch biopsy of an active lesion sent for direct immunofluorescence studies","Potassium hydroxide preparation of a plucked hair examined under a microscope","Skin scraping of an unexcoriated burrow with mineral oil on a number 15 blade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — microscopic identification of the organism, ova or faeces is the definitive diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"An 81-year-old nursing home resident is confirmed to have crusted scabies. Which is the most important consequence to act on?","choices":["The scale contains millions of mites, so the resident is highly infectious to staff and other residents","The absence of itch means the diagnosis is unlikely, so an alternative should be sought first","The thickened nails indicate a fungal infection, so a potassium hydroxide test is needed","The condition resolves on its own, so isolation and contact tracing are not required"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a hospital epidemic can follow admission, and infested healthcare workers make it hard to eradicate. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has mild pubic itching, slate-grey to bluish irregular macules about 1 cm across on the lower abdomen, and papules around the umbilicus. Which additional step is most appropriate?","choices":["Screen for concurrent sexually transmitted infection, since pubic lice often coexist with one","Screen for diabetes, since the intertriginous distribution suggests an underlying metabolic cause","Screen for immunosuppression, since the massive mite burden indicates impaired immunity","Screen for coeliac disease, since the eruption is associated with gluten sensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the macules are maculae caeruleae, representing haemorrhage at feeding sites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
